+\subsection{Direct discrete frequencies require no principal-density approximation}
+\label{gtl:direct-proof}
+For each original dual ideal, its good valuations determine
+uniquely $\mathfrak y=\mathfrak w\mathfrak s^6$ with good
+$\mathfrak s$ and good part of $\mathfrak w$ sixth-free.
+At $V=X^{1/6}/2$, the lower-open condition
+$N\mathfrak s>V$ implies $N\mathfrak w<64$.
+Conversely every ideal of norm below $64$ has sixth-free
+good part, since the least good prime norm is seven.
+This proves the exact support~\eqref{gtl:discrete-support}
+with all its nonunit $S$ modes and possible overlaps.
+No squarefreeness of $\mathfrak s$ or new coprimality with
+$\mathfrak k$ is introduced.
+
+Equation~\eqref{gtl:sixth-phase} holds for \emph{every}
+original $\rho$. Retaining that factor and the actual
+discrete $\mathfrak s$ family in~\eqref{cbk:exact-kernel}
+gives~\eqref{gtl:direct-prefix}. The prefactor is exactly
+$A_1/(D\sqrt F)$, including the once-only normalization.
+All primitive factors at $\mathfrak e,\mathfrak w$ are
+still separate, and a good collision remains zero
+even when its exponent is a sixth power.
+
+The pointwise real-axis remainder~\eqref{gtl:real-remainder}
+at $J=0$ implies
+\begin{equation}\label{gtl:direct-Bessel}
+ |\dot G(x)-g_0|
+    \le C_0^{-3/2}x\int_0^\infty vG(v)\,dv
+    \le3C_0^{-3/2}x,\qquad x\ge0 .
+\end{equation}
+Every argument in~\eqref{gtl:direct-prefix} is at most
+$\Lambda=A_1X/D^2$. Count only in this error:
+there are $O(D^2)$ annular column pairs,
+$A_1/(D\sqrt F)\le A_1/D^2$, the original coefficient
+products are $O(B_W^2D^\epsilon)$, and the original
+finite $\mathfrak k$ absolute budget is bounded by
+$\sum(N\mathfrak k)^{-2}$.
+The $\mathfrak e$/source/ray and $\mathfrak w$ sets have
+fixed finite mass. Since $V\ge1$, the exact good
+$\mathfrak s$ annulus contains $O(V)$ ideals.
+This proves~\eqref{gtl:direct-model-error}, without a
+ray count, interpolated endpoint, density or arbitrary
+row-weight norm.
+
+It remains to justify applying the column-tail theorem
+to every original ray. The good-$q$ powers in the proof
+of~\eqref{gtl:root-cubic} are local powers $1,2,3$ of
+$\theta_q$; that proof never uses $\rho^6=1$.
+For every original component the total $\eta$ is type
+zero and its finite $\theta_S$ is fixed at its actual
+primitive $S$ conductor. Hence the finite selector
+expansion in~\eqref{gtl:finite-selectors} and its
+coefficient mass are valid on all these components.
+No new assertion of sixth order for an individual
+$\xi$ or replacement of a primitive $S$ Gauss sum is made.
+
+Now freeze $\mathfrak s$ as well as
+$\mathfrak n,\mathfrak k,\mathfrak e,\mathfrak w$.
+Relative to Section~\ref{gtl:phase-proof}, the only new
+$\mathfrak m$ factor is
+$\mathbf1_{(\mathfrak m,\mathfrak s)=1}$.
+It is a bounded multiplicative ideal function and
+splits exactly in the grouped column as
+\begin{equation}\label{gtl:s-mask-split}
+ \mathbf1_{(\mathfrak a\mathfrak z,\mathfrak s)=1}
+   =\mathbf1_{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak s)=1}
+      \mathbf1_{(\mathfrak z,\mathfrak s)=1}.
+\end{equation}
+The $\mathfrak n/\mathfrak s$ mask is a scalar which may
+be zero. The fixed $\bar\rho(\mathfrak s)^6$ phase is
+another bounded scalar. All earlier full-union raw
+deletions and separate primitive $S$ values remain.
+Thus~\eqref{gtl:linear-tail} is uniform in
+$\mathfrak s$ as well as in $\mathfrak n,\mathfrak k$,
+with no $(N\mathfrak s)^\epsilon$ or
+$(N\mathfrak k)^\epsilon$ cost. Their growing deleted
+primes are coefficient diagonals in the same physical
+cubic matrix.
+
+There is no density in the new exact discrete model.
+Use the underlying arbitrary-$\lambda$ theorem with
+the factor $g(\mathfrak m)$ omitted, rather than dividing
+the earlier density-weighted moment by $g(q)$.
+If a $\mathfrak k$ domain has been written with
+$q$ deletion, first express it on the common original
+good-SF set $N\mathfrak k\le K$ with its exact raw
+$\eta(\mathfrak k)^2$ zero. This permits a fixed
+$\mathfrak k$ column argument, restores no prime and
+adds no $(\mathfrak k,\mathfrak s)$ condition.
+
+Apply the signed column estimate before the other
+column triangle. The latter is~\eqref{gtl:other-column};
+after once-only $1/D$ it yields
+$B_W\mathcal M(W)D^\epsilon
+ (Y^{-1/2}+D^{-1/6})$ for each fixed
+$\mathfrak s,\mathfrak w,\mathfrak k,\mathfrak e$
+and finite component, with the original $A_1g_0$ factor.
+Sum the original finite $\mathfrak k$ norm weights and
+the fixed $\mathfrak e,\mathfrak w$ components.
+Only now use the $O(V)$ count of the exact
+$\mathfrak s$ family. Reversed tails use conjugation,
+as in Section~\ref{gtl:reassembly-proof}, and the
+original unequal-norm selector uses the same separate
+fixed-norm fibre bound~\eqref{gtl:equal-fibre-cost}.
+The exact identity
+\begin{equation}\label{gtl:direct-bilinearity}
+ \begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal L_X^K[A_U,A_U]-\mathcal L_X^K[A_Y,A_Y]
+  &=\mathcal L_X^K[A_U-A_Y,A_U]\\
+  &\quad+\mathcal L_X^K[A_Y,A_U-A_Y]
+ \end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+then proves~\eqref{gtl:direct-model-tail}.
+Combining it with the one Bessel error proves
+\eqref{gtl:direct-bound}.
+
+This direct proof does not assert a smaller value for
+the separate $37/48$ certificate in the continuous
+mean route; it never introduces that split.
+All of its nonprincipal-ray and exact-discrete effects
+remain in $\mathcal L_X^K[A_Y,A_Y]$.
+At $Y\le D^{1/8}$ the residual column norms are at least
+$D/Y^2\ge D^{3/4}$, just as in~\eqref{gtl:long-residual}.
+The new model is still unbounded at target size.
+It is different from the principal-density
+$\mathcal L_0[A_Y,A_Y]$.
+
+A capacity-empty integer interval, or empty exact
+$\mathcal T_X$, gives zero by definition.
+Absence of actual good squarefree rows in a
+\emph{nonempty} integer interval alone does not imply
+that each dual prefix piece is zero; cancellation
+with other frequencies can be required.
+No full dual complement, other block, whole signed
+assembly or determinant/zero-location obligation is
+resolved by this direct small-cutoff reduction.
+
